--TEST--
A channel's type argument is the type of the queue in both directions: `send` takes that type and no other, the `foreach` binding declares that same type, and a class the channel was not declared with is refused as any other mismatch is — `rule:concurrency/one-scheduler`, `rule:types/grammar`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:concurrency/core-task-channel-t-a-bounded-queue-between-tasks
// A channel is the one place a value crosses between two tasks, so the type it carries
// is checked where it is written rather than where it is read: a queue that accepted
// anything would move the mismatch into the consumer, which is a different task and a
// different line.
class Order {}
class Refund {}

var $numbers = new Core\Task\Channel<int>(2);

// Going in: the argument is the declared element type.
$numbers->send("12");

// Coming out: the `foreach` binding is that same type, so it cannot narrow or widen it.
foreach ($numbers as string $line) {
    echo $line, "\n";
}

var $orders = new Core\Task\Channel<Order>(2);

// A class is no weaker a bound than a scalar: `Refund` is not `Order`.
$orders->send(new Refund());

foreach ($orders as Order $order) {
    echo "one order\n";
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `int`, found `string`
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
